# Valgrind suppressions for tools/test-valgrind.sh and the libzpdf bundle
# (tools/valgrind-abi-bundle.sh). Each one names the exact frames it hides and why
# the report is not a defect.

# std 0.17 lib/std/compress/flate/Compress.zig, huffman.buildValues: for every
# symbol it reads base[bits]. A symbol with bit length 0 has frequency 0 and
# base[0] is never set, so its code is undefined; such a symbol is never
# written. std says so there: "For `b == 0` this is fine since v is specified
# to be undefined." zpdf's tests inflate every compressed stream and compare it
# with the uncompressed one, which would fail if an undefined code were used.
{
   std-flate-huffman-code-of-unused-symbol
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*huffman.buildValues
   fun:*huffman.build
}

# std 0.17 lib/std/heap/SafeAllocator.zig, AllocFooter.Modify.setNone: an atomic exchange
# of a 16-bit field in an allocation's footer. A Debug build for aarch64 lowers the exchange to a
# compare-and-swap loop: it reads the old value, then compares it with what the exclusive load
# reads. When a resize writes a footer into memory the allocator has marked undefined (a valgrind
# client request), both values are undefined and memcheck reports the two branches of that loop.
# The value stored does not depend on the old one. A program with no zpdf code reproduces it: malloc
# two bytes, write them, mark them undefined with std.valgrind.memcheck.makeMemUndefined, and
# @atomicRmw(u16, p, .Xchg, 0x2962, .monotonic) gives the same two reports; without the mark,
# none. zpdf meets it through ArenaAllocator resizes in the instancer tests.
{
   std-safe-allocator-footer-exchange
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*SafeAllocator.AllocFooter.Modify.setNone
}
